<article class="env-lemma" id="07VF">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07VF">Lemma <span data-tag="07VF">15.4.2</span>.</a> Assumptions as in Lemma <a href="/tag/07VE" data-tag="07VE">15.4.1</a>. Let $Q = \mathop{\mathrm{Coker}}(g)$ and $Q' = \mathop{\mathrm{Coker}}(g')$. Then $\text{Gr}_ I(Q) \cong \text{Gr}_ I(Q')$ as graded $\text{Gr}_ I(A)$-modules. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      In degree $n$ we have $\text{Gr}_ I(Q)_ n = I^ nN/(I^{n + 1}N + g(M) \cap I^ nN)$ and similarly for $Q'$. We claim that 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  g(M) \cap I^ nN \subset I^{n + 1}N + g'(M) \cap I^ nN.  \]
</div>
    <p>
       By symmetry (the proof of the claim will only use that $c$ works for $g$ which also holds for $g'$ by the lemma) this will imply that 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  I^{n + 1}N + g(M) \cap I^ nN = I^{n + 1}N + g'(M) \cap I^ nN  \]
</div>
    <p>
       whence $\text{Gr}_ I(Q)_ n$ and $\text{Gr}_ I(Q')_ n$ agree as subquotients of $N$, implying the lemma. Observe that the claim is clear for $n \leq c$ as $g = g' \bmod I^{c + 1}N$. If $n > c$, then suppose $b \in g(M) \cap I^ nN$. Write $b = g(a)$ for $a \in I^{n - c}M$. Set $b' = g'(a)$. We have $b - b' = (g - g')(a) \in I^{n + 1}N$ as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>